Subject: wasix: suspend on interruptible host futexes

A guest futex wait went through the async path: a logical waiter in
the futex table, a Tokio timer for the timeout, and the coroutine
suspended on a future for the host loop to poll. For a guest whose
idle threads wait on futexes with short timeouts, that machinery was
most of the host's remaining CPU.

Let an embedder do the wait in the kernel instead. Its hooks take a
wait on the WASI thread's interrupt generation and the freeze-gate
generation until an absolute deadline, and on a guest futex word with
its expected value when the caller has one. A process-local futex in a
linear memory whose base cannot move is handed over that way. The
kernel checks the values and enqueues the waiter atomically, so no
logical waiter is registered. A signal or a termination bumps and
wakes the thread's generation, and closing the freeze gate bumps and
wakes the gate's; either returns the wait as interrupted, and the
syscall falls through to the existing path for the full signal and
termination processing. A wake goes to direct waiters first and to a
logical waiter only when none woke.

Shared-file futexes and dynamic memories keep the async path, since a
direct wait needs a word whose address cannot change. A coroutine
frozen in a direct wait retains it, so a resurrected child resumes the
same wait against its own gate generation. The hooks live behind
wasix's host-futex feature, which every native configuration enables,
and nothing changes until an embedder installs them and enables
suspension.

